Q: Getting 405 Error when Login button is clicked A: <p>There's no <code>#submitSignUp</code> element in your HTML so this line...</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// Functionality for the sign-up button
const signUp = document.getElementById(&quot;submitSignUp&quot;);
signUp.addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, (event) =&gt; {
</code></pre>
<p>will cause an error and none of the rest of your code will execute. In particular, the <code>event.preventDefault()</code> for your sign-in button click. Without that, your <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> submits normally via a POST request.</p>
<p>You'd need to check that the elements exist before attempting to add event listeners when this script is potentially added to multiple pages. For example</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// Functionality for the sign-up button
const signUp = document.getElementById(&quot;submitSignUp&quot;);

if (signUp) {
  signUp.addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, (event) =&gt; {
    // ...
  });
}
</code></pre>

Q: Django: User Experience for checking if a person already exists in the database (first and last name, and birthday) A: <p>The way I see, the main problem is to ensure the data remains the same during these steps as it can be altered midflow. And for that some kind of caching is necessary, to compare them at the end. That is the safest approach.</p>
<p>With that being said and persistence aside, a more advanced way would be to send a fetch request, filter Users and send back a response with similar results, then manipulate the DOM to display the wanted behaviour. And, in the same way either approve the insertion or cancel it.</p>
<p>A short example:</p>
<p>views.py</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>def collect_info(request):
    if request.method == &quot;POST&quot;:
        form = UserInput(request.POST)

        if form.is_valid():
            qs = **your lookup here
            filtered_fields = [
                model_to_dict(item, fields=[&quot;first_name&quot;, &quot;last_name&quot;, &quot;bday&quot;])
                for item in qs
            ]
            data = json.dumps(filtered_fields, default=str)

            if qs:
                return JsonResponse(
                    {&quot;original_entry&quot;: form.cleaned_data, &quot;similar_result&quot;: data}
                )

            else:
                return JsonResponse(
                    {&quot;original_entry&quot;: form.cleaned_data, &quot;similar_result&quot;: None}
                )
    else:
        form = UserInput()

    return render(request, &quot;index.html&quot;, {&quot;form&quot;: form})


def approve(request):
    if request.method == &quot;POST&quot;:
        form = UserInput(request.POST)

        if form.is_valid():
            partial_data = form.cleaned_data
            User.objects.create(**complete_data)
            return JsonResponse({&quot;message&quot;: &quot;User created&quot;})
        else:
            return JsonResponse({&quot;message&quot;: &quot;Invalid data&quot;})

    else:
        return JsonResponse({&quot;message&quot;: &quot;Method not allowed&quot;})
</code></pre>
<p>A template (note data attributes)</p>
<pre><code>    &lt;form id=&quot;lookup-form&quot; method=&quot;post&quot;&gt;
      {% csrf_token %} {{ form }}
      &lt;button id=&quot;check-input&quot; type=&quot;submit&quot; data-url=&quot;{% url 'collect' %}&quot;&gt;
        Check or Save
      &lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/form&gt;
    &lt;div id=&quot;lookup-result&quot; data-url=&quot;{% url 'approve' %}&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Lastly, the script:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>  &lt;script&gt;
    const lookupForm = document.getElementById(&quot;lookup-form&quot;);
    const submitButton = document.getElementById(&quot;check-input&quot;);
    const lookupResult = document.getElementById(&quot;lookup-result&quot;);

    submitButton.addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, submitLookupFormHandler);

    function buildApprovalButtons() {
      const wrapper = document.createElement(&quot;div&quot;);
      const title = document.createElement(&quot;h2&quot;);
      title.innerHTML = &quot;is this a duplicate of any of these other people?&quot;;

      const yesButton = document.createElement(&quot;button&quot;);
      yesButton.innerHTML = &quot;Yes&quot;;
      yesButton.addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, () =&gt; {
        console.log(&quot;return somewhere, or just clean everything idk&quot;);
      });

      const noButton = document.createElement(&quot;button&quot;);
      noButton.innerHTML = &quot;No&quot;;
      noButton.addEventListener(&quot;click&quot;, submitApprovalHandler);

      wrapper.appendChild(title);
      wrapper.appendChild(yesButton);
      wrapper.appendChild(noButton);

      lookupResult.appendChild(wrapper);
    }

    function buildResult(data) {
      const wrapper = document.createElement(&quot;div&quot;);
      const firstName = document.createElement(&quot;p&quot;);
      const lastName = document.createElement(&quot;p&quot;);
      const birthDate = document.createElement(&quot;p&quot;);

      firstName.innerHTML = `${data.first_name}`;
      lastName.innerHTML = `${data.last_name}`;
      birthDate.innerHTML = `${data.bday}`;

      wrapper.appendChild(firstName);
      wrapper.appendChild(lastName);
      wrapper.appendChild(birthDate);

      lookupResult.appendChild(wrapper);
      const divider = document.createElement(&quot;hr&quot;);
      lookupResult.appendChild(divider);
    }

    async function submitLookupFormHandler(event) {
      event.preventDefault();
      lookupResult.innerHTML = &quot;&quot;;
      const formData = new FormData(lookupForm);
      const url = submitButton.dataset.url;

      r = await fetch(url, { method: &quot;POST&quot;, body: formData });

      if (r.status == 200) {
        const data = await r.json();
        if (data.similar_result) {
          const similarResults = JSON.parse(data.similar_result);
          similarResults.map((result) =&gt; {
            buildResult(result);
          });
          buildApprovalButtons();
        } else {
          lookupResult.innerHTML = &quot;no results were found&quot;;
        }
      }
    }

    async function submitApprovalHandler() {
        // Send another request
    }
  &lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: How do I programmatically handle swipe-to-scroll behavior? A: <p>I have resolved the issue after some testing and minor code modifications. I found that I need to adjust the sensitivity specifically for the swipe-to-scroll functionality (i.e., the <code>touchmove</code> event), as the desktop scrolling behavior is working correctly. Here's my working minor code modification:</p>
<pre><code>container.on(&quot;touchmove&quot;, function (e) {
  if (container.parent().is(&quot;.not-scrollable&quot;)) {
    return true;
  }

  const touchEndY = e.originalEvent.touches[0].clientY;
  const deltaMovingY = getMaxScrollY() &gt; 200 ? 10 : 100;
  const deltaY = touchStartY - touchEndY; // Determine swipe direction

  // Sensitivity control (lower = smoother)
  const scrollSpeed = 0.1; // adjust as necessary 

  if (deltaY &gt; 0) {
    move($(`#${scroll_thumb_id}`)[0], 0, deltaMovingY * scrollSpeed); // Scroll down
  } else {
    move($(`#${scroll_thumb_id}`)[0], 0, -deltaMovingY * scrollSpeed); // Scroll up
  }

  touchStartY = touchEndY; // Update for smooth scrolling

  e.preventDefault(); // Prevent default scrolling
});
</code></pre>
<p>On my end, these lines of code fixed the issue:</p>
<pre><code>const scrollSpeed = 0.1;
</code></pre>
<pre><code>move($(`#${scroll_thumb_id}`)[0], 0, deltaMovingY * scrollSpeed);
</code></pre>
<pre><code>move($(`#${scroll_thumb_id}`)[0], 0, -deltaMovingY * scrollSpeed);
</code></pre>
<p>This change makes the mobile and tablet swipe-to-scroll behavior smoother and slower, as desired.</p>
